// =============================================================================
//  Hospital Patient Visits -- DAX library
// =============================================================================
//
//  One DAX definition for every figure the T-SQL layer publishes, so the report
//  and the warehouse cannot drift. Each block names the query it mirrors; run
//  that query (or open exports/qNN_*.csv) to see the number this should return.
//
//  Order matters. Build the Date table first, then the calculated columns, then
//  the measures -- later definitions reference earlier ones.
//
//  Two ways to load it:
//    * By hand, in Power BI Desktop. Modeling > New measure, paste one block.
//    * In bulk, with Tabular Editor 2 (free). See powerbi/README.md.
//
//  Conventions used throughout:
//    * DIVIDE(), never "/". DIVIDE returns BLANK on a zero denominator, which
//      is the DAX equivalent of the NULLIF() guards in the SQL.
//    * KEEPFILTERS() on predicate filters, so a CALCULATE narrows the user's
//      selection instead of replacing it.
//    * Percentages are returned as ratios (0.2000, not 20.00) and formatted as
//      percent in the model. The CSVs store them as 20.00 -- same number, one
//      is presentation.
//
// =============================================================================


// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  1. Date table                                          calculated table
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  Nothing in exports/ is a date dimension, and time intelligence will not work
//  without one: DAX needs a contiguous, gap-free date column it can mark as the
//  model's date table. Generating it here beats shipping another CSV, because
//  it cannot fall out of sync with the fact table's range.
//
//  After creating it: Table tools > Mark as date table > Date.

Date =
VAR Days =
    CALENDAR ( DATE ( 2020, 1, 1 ), DATE ( 2025, 12, 31 ) )
RETURN
    ADDCOLUMNS (
        Days,
        "Year", YEAR ( [Date] ),
        "Month Number", MONTH ( [Date] ),
        "Month Name", FORMAT ( [Date], "MMM" ),
        "Month Start", DATE ( YEAR ( [Date] ), MONTH ( [Date] ), 1 ),
        "Year Month", FORMAT ( [Date], "YYYY-MM" ),
        "Day Name", FORMAT ( [Date], "ddd" ),
        "Day Type", IF ( WEEKDAY ( [Date], 2 ) >= 6, "Weekend", "Weekday" )
    )

//  WEEKDAY's second argument is the week-start convention, and it is the whole
//  ballgame here. Type 2 numbers Monday as 1 through Sunday as 7, so ">= 6" is
//  Saturday and Sunday. Type 1 (the default) numbers Sunday as 1, which would
//  quietly classify Sunday as a weekday and Friday as a weekend. The SQL uses
//  DATEPART(WEEKDAY, ...) under SET DATEFIRST 1, which is the same convention
//  as type 2. Expect 14,283 weekend visits; if you get a different number, the
//  convention is wrong.

//  Set these after the table exists:
//    'Date'[Month Name]  Sort by column -> 'Date'[Month Number]
//    'Date'[Year Month]  Sort by column -> 'Date'[Month Start]
//    'Date'[Day Name]    Sort by column -> a WEEKDAY([Date], 2) column if you
//                        add one; otherwise the axis sorts alphabetically.


// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  2. Calculated columns on 'Fact Visits'
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  Age has to be a column, not a measure: it is an attribute of the visit that
//  the user slices by. It also has to be age *at the visit*, not age today --
//  a patient born in 2011 was a child for their 2020 visits and is not one for
//  their 2025 ones. Mirrors q05_age_band.sql.

Age At Visit =
VAR Birth = RELATED ( 'Patient'[DOB] )
VAR Visit = 'Fact Visits'[VisitDate]
VAR ElapsedYears = YEAR ( Visit ) - YEAR ( Birth )
VAR BirthdayHasPassed =
    MONTH ( Visit ) > MONTH ( Birth )
        || ( MONTH ( Visit ) = MONTH ( Birth ) && DAY ( Visit ) >= DAY ( Birth ) )
RETURN
    ElapsedYears - IF ( BirthdayHasPassed, 0, 1 )

//  DATEDIFF ( Birth, Visit, YEAR ) is the tempting one-liner and it is wrong:
//  it counts calendar-year boundaries crossed, so 2000-12-31 to 2001-01-01 comes
//  back as one year. The subtraction above is the birthday-adjusted rule the
//  SQL and the Python oracle both use.


Age Band =
SWITCH (
    TRUE (),
    'Fact Visits'[Age At Visit] < 18, "0-17",
    'Fact Visits'[Age At Visit] <= 35, "18-35",
    'Fact Visits'[Age At Visit] <= 55, "36-55",
    "56+"
)


//  Without this, every age axis in the report reads 0-17, 18-35, 36-55, 56+ in
//  alphabetical order, which puts 0-17 first by luck and 56+ second by mistake.

Age Band Order =
SWITCH (
    'Fact Visits'[Age Band],
    "0-17", 1,
    "18-35", 2,
    "36-55", 3,
    4
)

//  Then: select 'Fact Visits'[Age Band], Sort by column -> [Age Band Order].


// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  3. Core volume and money                       folder: 01 Core
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  q01_operating_kpis.sql

Total Visits =
COUNTROWS ( 'Fact Visits' )


Distinct Patients =
DISTINCTCOUNT ( 'Fact Visits'[PatientID] )


Total Revenue =
SUM ( 'Fact Visits'[BillAmount] )


Insurance Covered =
SUM ( 'Fact Visits'[InsuranceAmount] )


//  What the patient is actually out of pocket for -- the gap the finance side
//  cares about and the only figure here that is a difference of two others.

Patient Paid =
[Total Revenue] - [Insurance Covered]


Insurance Coverage % =
DIVIDE ( [Insurance Covered], [Total Revenue] )


Average Bill =
DIVIDE ( [Total Revenue], [Total Visits] )


Average Wait Minutes =
AVERAGE ( 'Fact Visits'[WaitTimeMinutes] )


Average Satisfaction =
AVERAGE ( 'Fact Visits'[SatisfactionScore] )

//  AVERAGE returns BLANK over an empty selection rather than erroring, so these
//  two need no DIVIDE guard.


// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  4. Service quality                             folder: 02 Service
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  q07_department_service_risk.sql

Seen Within 30 Min =
CALCULATE (
    [Total Visits],
    KEEPFILTERS ( 'Fact Visits'[WaitTimeMinutes] <= 30 )
)


Seen Within 30 Min % =
DIVIDE ( [Seen Within 30 Min], [Total Visits] )


Satisfied Visits =
CALCULATE (
    [Total Visits],
    KEEPFILTERS ( 'Fact Visits'[SatisfactionScore] >= 4 )
)


Satisfied % =
DIVIDE ( [Satisfied Visits], [Total Visits] )


Median Wait Minutes =
MEDIAN ( 'Fact Visits'[WaitTimeMinutes] )


Wait P10 Minutes =
PERCENTILE.INC ( 'Fact Visits'[WaitTimeMinutes], 0.10 )


Wait P90 Minutes =
PERCENTILE.INC ( 'Fact Visits'[WaitTimeMinutes], 0.90 )

//  PERCENTILE.INC interpolates between the two neighbouring values; the web
//  dashboard uses the nearest-rank convention instead, because it reads its
//  percentiles off an integer histogram. On 50,000 rows the two agree to within
//  a minute, but they are different definitions and will not always tie out to
//  the decimal. Pick one per report and say which.


//  The triage board's colour rule, ported so the Power BI matrix can use the
//  same ramp. Each threshold is where the seeded data drops a satisfaction star.
//  Use it under Conditional formatting > Background colour > Field value.

Wait Band Colour =
VAR Wait = [Average Wait Minutes]
RETURN
    SWITCH (
        TRUE (),
        ISBLANK ( Wait ), "#DEE6E3",
        Wait <= 25, "#2E7D74",
        Wait <= 40, "#7CAE9E",
        Wait <= 55, "#C9A227",
        Wait <= 70, "#C77B2B",
        "#AE3B49"
    )


// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  5. Growth                                      folder: 03 Growth
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  q02_annual_growth.sql -- the SQL is LAG(...) OVER (ORDER BY VisitYear), so
//  the comparison is year bucket against year bucket.
//
//  Note what this deliberately does NOT use. The textbook answer is
//
//      Visits LY = CALCULATE ( [Total Visits], DATEADD ( 'Date'[Date], -1, YEAR ) )
//
//  and on this dataset it is wrong. DATEADD shifts each date back individually,
//  and 2021 has no 29 February to map 2020's onto, so the twelve visits dated
//  2020-02-29 vanish from the comparison. 2021 growth comes out at 20.29% instead
//  of 20.00%. SAMEPERIODLASTYEAR has the same hole. Filtering the year column
//  outright is both simpler and exactly what the LAG does.

Visits LY =
VAR PriorYear = SELECTEDVALUE ( 'Date'[Year] ) - 1
RETURN
    CALCULATE (
        [Total Visits],
        REMOVEFILTERS ( 'Date' ),
        'Date'[Year] = PriorYear
    )


Visit Growth % =
DIVIDE ( [Total Visits] - [Visits LY], [Visits LY] )


Revenue LY =
VAR PriorYear = SELECTEDVALUE ( 'Date'[Year] ) - 1
RETURN
    CALCULATE (
        [Total Revenue],
        REMOVEFILTERS ( 'Date' ),
        'Date'[Year] = PriorYear
    )


Revenue Growth % =
DIVIDE ( [Total Revenue] - [Revenue LY], [Revenue LY] )


//  q09_monthly_trend.sql -- the windowed running total.
//  ALLSELECTED, not ALL: the line should restart when the user slices to a year
//  rather than always climbing from January 2020.

Cumulative Visits =
VAR LastDateInContext = MAX ( 'Date'[Date] )
RETURN
    CALCULATE (
        [Total Visits],
        FILTER (
            ALLSELECTED ( 'Date'[Date] ),
            'Date'[Date] <= LastDateInContext
        )
    )


Cumulative Revenue =
VAR LastDateInContext = MAX ( 'Date'[Date] )
RETURN
    CALCULATE (
        [Total Revenue],
        FILTER (
            ALLSELECTED ( 'Date'[Date] ),
            'Date'[Date] <= LastDateInContext
        )
    )


// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  6. Mix and rank                                folder: 04 Mix
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  q04_payment_mix.sql -- share of whatever the visual is currently showing.
//  ALLSELECTED means the denominator respects slicers but ignores the row, so
//  the column totals 100% on any breakdown you drop it into.

Revenue Share % =
DIVIDE (
    [Total Revenue],
    CALCULATE ( [Total Revenue], ALLSELECTED () )
)


Visit Share % =
DIVIDE (
    [Total Visits],
    CALCULATE ( [Total Visits], ALLSELECTED () )
)


//  q06_department_rank.sql -- revenue rank inside the department's own category,
//  which is what the SQL's PARTITION BY DepartmentCategory does.
//  DENSE, to match DENSE_RANK: tied departments share a rank and the next rank
//  is not skipped.

Revenue Rank in Category =
VAR PeerDepartments =
    CALCULATETABLE (
        VALUES ( 'Department'[DepartmentName] ),
        ALLSELECTED ( 'Department' ),
        VALUES ( 'Department'[DepartmentCategory] )
    )
RETURN
    IF (
        HASONEVALUE ( 'Department'[DepartmentName] ),
        RANKX ( PeerDepartments, [Total Revenue],, DESC, DENSE )
    )


Category Revenue Share % =
DIVIDE (
    [Total Revenue],
    CALCULATE (
        [Total Revenue],
        ALLEXCEPT ( 'Department', 'Department'[DepartmentCategory] )
    )
)


//  q08_weekday_weekend.sql

Weekend Visits =
CALCULATE ( [Total Visits], KEEPFILTERS ( 'Date'[Day Type] = "Weekend" ) )


Weekend Share % =
DIVIDE ( [Weekend Visits], [Total Visits] )


// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  7. Patients and doctors                        folder: 05 People
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  q11_repeat_patients.sql
//
//  Iterating the Patient dimension rather than the fact table is deliberate.
//  Relationships run one way here (dimension filters fact), so [Total Visits]
//  evaluated against a patient row returns that patient's visits *within the
//  current slice*. Patients with none come back BLANK, and BLANK > 1 is false,
//  so they drop out on their own. Slice to Cardiology and this correctly becomes
//  "patients who came to Cardiology more than once".

Repeat Patients =
COUNTROWS (
    FILTER (
        VALUES ( 'Patient'[PatientID] ),
        [Total Visits] > 1
    )
)


Repeat Patient Rate % =
DIVIDE ( [Repeat Patients], [Distinct Patients] )


Visits per Patient =
DIVIDE ( [Total Visits], [Distinct Patients] )


//  q03_doctor_patient_load.sql

Active Doctors =
DISTINCTCOUNT ( 'Fact Visits'[DoctorID] )


Patients per Doctor =
DIVIDE ( [Distinct Patients], [Active Doctors] )


Visits per Doctor =
DIVIDE ( [Total Visits], [Active Doctors] )


//  q10_top_doctors.sql -- rank by satisfaction among doctors with at least 100
//  visits. The HAVING clause survives here as the [Total Visits] >= 100 guard,
//  applied twice: once to build the peer set, once to blank out the doctors who
//  should not appear at all.

Doctor Performance Rank =
VAR EligibleDoctors =
    FILTER (
        CALCULATETABLE ( VALUES ( 'Doctor'[DoctorID] ), ALLSELECTED ( 'Doctor' ) ),
        [Total Visits] >= 100
    )
RETURN
    IF (
        HASONEVALUE ( 'Doctor'[DoctorID] ) && [Total Visits] >= 100,
        RANKX ( EligibleDoctors, [Average Satisfaction],, DESC, DENSE )
    )


//  The measure above ranks on satisfaction alone, so it reproduces the SQL's
//  primary sort but not its tie-breaks -- q10 breaks ties on wait time ascending,
//  then visit count descending. RANKX takes one expression, so the trick is to
//  fold the tie-break into it.
//
//  Satisfaction is a mean of integers 1..5 over at least 100 visits, so two
//  distinct doctors' averages differ by at least 1/308, about 0.0032. Waits run
//  12..88 minutes, so dividing by 100,000 perturbs the key by at most 0.00088 --
//  a quarter of the smallest real gap. The nudge can therefore reorder ties and
//  nothing else. Check that margin again if the visit floor or the wait range
//  ever changes.

Doctor Rank Key =
[Average Satisfaction] - DIVIDE ( [Average Wait Minutes], 100000 )


Doctor Performance Rank (exact) =
VAR EligibleDoctors =
    FILTER (
        CALCULATETABLE ( VALUES ( 'Doctor'[DoctorID] ), ALLSELECTED ( 'Doctor' ) ),
        [Total Visits] >= 100
    )
RETURN
    IF (
        HASONEVALUE ( 'Doctor'[DoctorID] ) && [Total Visits] >= 100,
        RANKX ( EligibleDoctors, [Doctor Rank Key],, DESC, DENSE )
    )


//  Drop this into the leaderboard's visual-level filter, "is less than or equal
//  to 10", to reproduce q10's WHERE PerformanceRank <= 10. Eleven rows is the
//  right answer, not a bug: two doctors share rank 6.


// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  8. Presentation                                folder: 06 Display
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//  Power BI's display units go thousand / million / billion. Indian reporting
//  goes lakh / crore, and a hospital board reading "1.58bn" would have to do the
//  conversion in their head. This returns text, so it belongs in cards and table
//  columns -- never on an axis, which needs a number to scale.

Revenue Label =
VAR Amount = [Total Revenue]
RETURN
    SWITCH (
        TRUE (),
        ISBLANK ( Amount ), "--",
        Amount >= 1E7, "₹" & FORMAT ( Amount / 1E7, "#,0.00" ) & " Cr",
        Amount >= 1E5, "₹" & FORMAT ( Amount / 1E5, "#,0.00" ) & " L",
        "₹" & FORMAT ( Amount, "#,0" )
    )


//  Cards show "50,000" with no hint of what is filtered. This spells it out.

Filter Context Label =
VAR Visits = [Total Visits]
VAR Share = FORMAT ( DIVIDE ( Visits, CALCULATE ( [Total Visits], REMOVEFILTERS () ) ), "0.0%" )
RETURN
    FORMAT ( Visits, "#,0" ) & " visits selected  ·  " & Share & " of all visits"


// =============================================================================
//  Formatting to set once the measures exist
// =============================================================================
//
//    Total Visits, Distinct Patients, Seen Within 30 Min,
//    Satisfied Visits, Repeat Patients, Weekend Visits,
//    Active Doctors, Cumulative Visits                 Whole number, #,0
//
//    Total Revenue, Insurance Covered, Patient Paid,
//    Average Bill, Cumulative Revenue                  Decimal, ₹#,0
//
//    Average Wait Minutes, Median Wait Minutes,
//    Wait P10 Minutes, Wait P90 Minutes,
//    Visits per Patient, Patients per Doctor,
//    Visits per Doctor                                 Decimal, #,0.0
//
//    Average Satisfaction                              Decimal, 0.00
//
//    every measure ending in %                         Percentage, 0.0%
//
//    Wait Band Colour                                  Text, and set
//                                                      Data category = Hex colour
//
// =============================================================================
